{"answer":"Let S_n=1+3+\u000b5+\u000b\\u2026+(2n-1). For n=1, S_1=1=1^2. Assume S_k=k^2 for some k\u001e\u0001. Then\nS_{k+1}=S_k+(2(k+1)-1)=k^2+2k+1=(k+1)^2.\nThus the identity holds for k+1 whenever it holds for k; together with the base case, it holds for every positive integer n.","approaches":[{"description":"Induction using the fact that the next odd summand is 2k+1.","id":"induction-proof","outcome":"candidate","reason":"The base case and induction step establish the stated identity for all positive integers."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by the base case and the discharged induction subproof in steps base-case, induction-step, and induction-conclusion.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["base-identity","induction-hypothesis"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"odd-sum-identity","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For every positive integer n, 1+3+5+\u000b…+(2n-1)=n^2.","step_ids":["base-case","induction-step","induction-conclusion"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Direct arithmetic in step base-case.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"base-identity","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The identity holds for n=1.","step_ids":["base-case"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"local_assumption","basis_reference":"Induction hypothesis introduced only for the subproof in steps induction-hypothesis and induction-step.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":["induction-step"],"id":"induction-hypothesis","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":["induction-hypothesis","induction-step"],"statement":"For an arbitrary positive integer k, assume 1+3+\u000b…+(2k-1)=k^2.","step_ids":["induction-hypothesis"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"base-case","justification":"The left-hand side is 1 and 1=1^2.","statement":"For n=1, 1=1^2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"induction-hypothesis","justification":"Introduce the induction hypothesis for an arbitrary positive integer k.","statement":"Assume 1+3+\u000b…+(2k-1)=k^2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["induction-hypothesis"],"id":"induction-step","justification":"Appending the next odd term gives k^2+(2k+1)=(k+1)^2. This discharges the induction hypothesis as a local assumption.","statement":"1+3+\u000b…+(2k-1)+(2(k+1)-1)=(k+1)^2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["base-case","induction-step"],"id":"induction-conclusion","justification":"The base case and the implication from k to k+1 establish the result for every positive integer n by induction.","statement":"The stated identity holds for every positive integer n."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}

